  "summary": "Four preprints recast topological invariants as readable from zeros, overlaps, and localizers rather than the bulk Hamiltonian alone.",
  "synthesis": "The four preprints share a dominant thread: topological invariants and boundary-protected modes are diagnosed through quantities other than the conventional bulk Hamiltonian spectrum. Arxiv 2611.25037 reads relative Chern and Z2 invariants of two gapped band structures from zeros of the occupied-subspace overlap determinant. Arxiv 2611.25028 shows that crystalline symmetry can enforce wavefunction zeros and vorticities characterizing Chern topology, readable from band-dispersion shifts. Arxiv 2611.26160 generalizes the spectral localizer to unify Z and Z2 invariants across hinge modes, Dirac nodes, and valley boundary modes. Arxiv 2611.19328 argues that edge modes of the non-Hermitian Loschmidt matrix, rather than Hamiltonian topology, protect boundary effects in dynamical quantum phase transitions. What is new is the shift of the diagnostic object to overlap and wavefunction zeros, localizer operators, and non-Hermitian Loschmidt matrices. The DQPT item is the least direct fit to this thread, since it concerns dynamics rather than ground-state band topology.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-20"
}
